Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Benefits of marriage as a search strategy", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838198037205736*^9, 
  3.838198060426086*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"45510674-881e-4046-9214-\
db7f80c897c4"],

Cell["by Davi B. Costa", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8381981938764887`*^9, 
  3.838198201427196*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"758d9d2a-0a12-47c3-8cdd-\
6f8ac672222e"],

Cell["\<\
We propose and investigate a model for dating and marriage in large societies \
based on a stochastic matching process and simple decision rules. Agents have \
preferences among themselves given by some probability distribution. They \
randomly search for better mates, forming new couples and breaking apart in \
the process. Marriage is implemented in the model by adding the decision of \
stopping searching for a better mate when an agent finds another one with an \
affinity higher than a certain fixed amount. We show that the average utility \
in the system with marriage can be higher than in the system without it. Part \
of our results can be summarized in what sounds like a piece of advice: don\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t marry the first person you like and \
don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t search for the love of your life, but get married if \
you like your partner more than a sigma above average. We also compare the \
evolution of the fraction of married couples in our model with real data and \
obtain good agreement. In the last section, we formulate the model in the \
limit of an infinite number of agents and find an analytical expression for \
the evolution of the system.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.83819816070051*^9, 3.8381981647629967`*^9}, {
  3.838198208219199*^9, 3.8381982166041517`*^9}, {3.8381983491747217`*^9, 
  3.838198355062541*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a6f558a4-02f2-4112-a074-\
3e06c0024745"],

Cell["\<\
(This Mathematica notebook is a companion to the article: \
https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04885. More details can be found in the article \
which is freely available in the Arxiv repository)\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8381979893183317`*^9, 3.8381980327769537`*^9}, {
  3.838198122244082*^9, 3.8381981535949163`*^9}, {3.8381986237718287`*^9, 
  3.838198627427134*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"605685ed-639c-426f-acca-\
b13fa79c8e07"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["1. Introduction", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838198265010025*^9, 3.838198276192183*^9}, {
  3.838199594326645*^9, 
  3.8381995948305798`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"bb70b459-fc04-4ccd-9f28-\
e4a2a6e9be63"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Consider the following situation: you are dating and wonder whether you \
should propose marriage or not. There are many aspects of marriage to \
consider, but you are mainly concerned with marriage as a search strategy. \
You like your mate, but you are unsure if it is enough. After all, there may \
be someone out there that you would like even more, but it is unlikely that \
you will get to know this person if you get married. Perhaps, the best is to \
stay open to the possibility of meeting a potential better mate by not \
getting married. However, if your relationship is symmetric in this respect, \
you may become single because your mate left you for someone else. So while \
marriage closes off some of the opportunities that you would otherwise have, \
on the other hand, it protects you from the risk of being left out. So you \
ask yourself: as a search strategy, is it beneficial to get married? Or does \
the potential benefit of meeting a person with whom you have a greater \
affinity outweigh the chances of being alone because your current partner has \
left you? To make these ideas precise and to answer the\nquestion, we propose \
a model for dating and marriage based on a random matching process and simple \
decision rules.\n\nThe model contains a number ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["N", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->"TraditionalForm",ExpressionUUID->
  "8f106a22-ef87-4856-8094-7ba51e61547f"],
 " of agents with preferences among themselves sampled by a given \
distribution. Initially, all agents are singles, and the system evolves in a \
certain number ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["T", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->"TraditionalForm",ExpressionUUID->
  "66655bcd-5fa0-4cac-bb9b-860ec1c84ba8"],
 " of discrete steps. At each step, they will match in pairs by random \
chance. If the agents of the matched pair like each other more than their \
previous mates, or more than being single, they will form a new couple. An \
agent becomes single if its partner starts a new relationship. Marriage is \
implemented in the model by adding the decision of stopping searching for a \
better mate when an agent finds another one with an affinity higher than a \
certain fixed value ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[CapitalLambda]", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->"TraditionalForm",ExpressionUUID->
  "10546d35-d010-4c5b-afe4-c342fe8102e3"],
 ". This parameter should be interpreted as exogenous in the sense that it \
characterizes the decision behavior of the agents of a given society with \
respect to marriage. We simulate this system for different values of \
\[CapitalLambda] and we computed the average utility in each case as a \
function of the number of steps. Part of the conclusions one can extract from \
the results we obtained, answers the question from the previous paragraph. \
The answer sounds like a piece of advice: don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t marry the \
first person you like and don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t search for the love of your \
life, but get married if you like your partner more than a sigma above \
average."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8381982861087837`*^9, 3.8381983439667892`*^9}, {
  3.8381988517821503`*^9, 3.83819885524646*^9}, {3.838199632318432*^9, 
  3.8381997254094677`*^9}, {3.838222806378173*^9, 
  3.838222813612577*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"cd7dc21f-468a-4f96-94a1-\
c60e393213e0"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["2. Model and results", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.835778392752408*^9, 3.835778397767687*^9}, {
  3.83586920352635*^9, 3.835869210046764*^9}, {3.8381995966703568`*^9, 
  3.838199597087008*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"de3c1cb7-e307-48ff-80c9-\
73a9177bc57a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["2.1. The model", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.83820629307612*^9, 3.838206303011878*^9}, 
   3.838213031949334*^9},ExpressionUUID->"624c83eb-d9a0-4b61-b4c5-\
bd640b5550a4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\tThe model contains ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5f71e6c4-9b7d-4a20-9c69-cd4f49bafc21"],
 " agents with preferences among themselves described by a utility function. \
This information is encoded in the off-diagonal elements of the affinity \
matrix ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"A", "\[Element]", 
    SuperscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]", 
     RowBox[{"n", " ", "x", " ", "n"}]]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "0e9e6f47-c82b-4bac-84a4-6892b85cd6f9"],
 ", where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["A", "ij"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c8b28449-5f9e-4f4c-947c-41edce7d45b3"],
 " is the utility for agent ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["i", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "394c2f4e-e946-4704-a6ce-2310a25857e8"],
 " to be in a couple with agent ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["j", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "0c42e3e1-4ab7-4081-b3e6-89182d013661"],
 ". In large societies, the probability distribution that generates the \
affinity matrix is more important than its actual values. Indeed, the \
preferences of agent ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "15ef76f0-bba8-4dfc-bfbd-87b33c933ee4"],
 " concerning the other agents of society are encoded by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox["A", "kj"], ":", 
         RowBox[{"1", "\[LessEqual]", "j", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}]}], " ", 
        "&"}], " ", "j"}], "\[NotEqual]", "k"}], "}"}], " "}], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"dbfeccf8-ef8f-4e02-ab99-1af4d4330669"],
 ". The histogram with these values, will distribute according to some \
probability distribution. As it happens with many human traits, it may be \
reasonable to assume that these values are generated by a Gaussian \
distribution, i.e., ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["A", "kj"], "\[Tilde]", 
    RowBox[{"N", "(", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", "k"], ",", 
      SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "k"]}], ")"}], " "}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"cc3a9fb2-f315-4196-b7e5-c5cbfbe98e89"],
 "for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"1", "\[LessEqual]", "j", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"1645397a-1ce1-4d7f-9a3c-09a2a41d335d"],
 " with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"j", "\[NotEqual]", "k"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7f7199d0-3d96-4a63-a258-566ae630328c"],
 " and each ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"1", "\[LessEqual]", "k", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"a18c50c5-40ed-4695-a02e-ff345a0bea23"],
 ". In principle, one may think that it is more realistic to assume that the \
preferences of different agents are given by distributions defined with \
different parameters \[Mu] and \[Sigma]. Note, however, that the values of \
the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "abf27dcc-13c8-4f83-a073-12f2491205c8"],
 "-column of the affinity matrix, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox["A", "jk"], ":", 
         RowBox[{"1", "\[LessEqual]", "j", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}]}], " ", 
        "&"}], " ", "j"}], "\[NotEqual]", "k"}], "}"}], " "}], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"f50e771e-d67a-435e-9f8c-a30972a2811a"],
 ", encodes the way agent ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "88f45900-705f-454b-bca9-2aaba0704d7e"],
 " is liked by society. Therefore, if agents indeed have preferences given by \
different Gaussian distributions, then the distribution that generates the \
columns of the affinity matrix will not in general be Gaussian. Hence, to \
have both lines and columns consistently Gaussian, we may assume that all \
off-diagonal entries of the affinity matrix are generated by the same \
Gaussian distribution, that is,  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["A", "kj"], "\[Tilde]", 
    RowBox[{"N", "(", 
     RowBox[{"\[Mu]", ",", "\[Sigma]"}], ")"}], " "}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"87b12511-5c69-4c2e-a3e9-c360da7ab191"],
 "for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"1", "\[LessEqual]", "j", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"c023dcc6-9bc8-4b19-b781-72b374127bc9"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"1", "\[LessEqual]", "k", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"bdb1eb3c-434f-41bc-9e50-9561fafed7e7"],
 " with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"j", "\[LessEqual]", "k"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "fa398b45-a478-4ddc-a905-ba2d269136f2"],
 ". When this is the case, we will say that we are dealing with the Gaussian \
model. For comparison and to make concrete our analytical investigations, we \
will also study the Uniform model, i.e., ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["A", "kj"], "\[Tilde]", 
    RowBox[{"U", "(", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "-", 
       RowBox[{"2", "\[Sigma]"}]}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "+", 
       RowBox[{"2", "\[Sigma]"}]}]}], ")"}], " "}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"2c873fff-b108-43da-8d0b-07234b34e716"],
 ".\n\tThe diagonal elements of the affinity matrix are the utilities when \
agents are singles. We also believe it is reasonable to assume these values \
are generated by a Gaussian distribution ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["A", "kk"], "\[Tilde]", 
    RowBox[{"N", "(", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", "s"], ",", 
      SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "s"]}], ")"}], " "}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"22afc05e-d56d-4bef-8054-79c02b6b446d"],
 "for all  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"1", "\[LessEqual]", "k", "\[LessEqual]", "n"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"6ae3139c-de69-48e4-8b1e-eb8d3eb2de91"],
 ". It is an open empirical problem to investigate the relation between the \
distributions that generate the diagonal and off-diagonal elements of the \
affinity matrix. In particular, it would be interesting to investigate the \
relationship between the mean of these distributions. Note, that the mean of \
the diagonal elements gives the average utility when all agents are single. \
On the other hand, the mean of the off-diagonal elements can be interpreted \
as the average utility when agents form random couples. Therefore, the \
relationship between the mean of these two distributions encodes how being \
single compares to dating a random agent in society. We denote the mean and \
variance of this distribution using an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["s", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "be3bd513-a17d-4aa0-bc93-0a1f823e06d6"],
 ".\n\tIn Wolfram Language we can generate the affinity matrix for the \
Gaussian model with parameters:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838200934052266*^9, 3.838200953963732*^9}, {
   3.838201022069448*^9, 3.8382010242748823`*^9}, {3.838201054849084*^9, 
   3.838201280326831*^9}, {3.838201314788246*^9, 3.838201341330347*^9}, {
   3.838201426190192*^9, 3.838201862941524*^9}, {3.838201911640874*^9, 
   3.838201983907756*^9}, 3.838202048443774*^9, {3.838202101245781*^9, 
   3.838202110901086*^9}, {3.83820218317949*^9, 3.838202209346743*^9}, {
   3.838202327055757*^9, 3.838202327616022*^9}, {3.8382023974545927`*^9, 
   3.838202414738317*^9}, {3.8382030880706997`*^9, 3.838203091504407*^9}, {
   3.838203165526909*^9, 3.838203192580072*^9}, {3.838292141319784*^9, 
   3.838292165136405*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"0e3b4012-0a45-481d-96d5-\
5dd0f0919fe7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"n", "=", "1000"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "=", "0"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "=", "1"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\[Mu]s", "=", "0"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"\[Sigma]s", "=", "1"}]}], "}"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838203038754137*^9, 3.838203059375153*^9}, 
   3.838203151181429*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[1]:=",ExpressionUUID->"94323c8c-e1f4-438e-ad5b-6864d07667ef"],

Cell["using", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838203187202056*^9, 
  3.838203188830998*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fb08be4d-1e6b-4b83-8c8e-\
d7ddfa3c3478"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"A", "=", 
    RowBox[{"RandomVariate", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"NormalDistribution", "[", 
       RowBox[{"\[Mu]", ",", "\[Sigma]"}], "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"n", ",", "n"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}], 
  " "}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Do", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"A", "\[LeftDoubleBracket]", "k", "\[RightDoubleBracket]"}], 
      "\[LeftDoubleBracket]", "k", "\[RightDoubleBracket]"}], "=", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"RandomVariate", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"NormalDistribution", "[", 
         RowBox[{"\[Mu]s", ",", "\[Sigma]s"}], "]"}], ",", "1"}], "]"}], 
      "\[LeftDoubleBracket]", "1", "\[RightDoubleBracket]"}]}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"k", ",", "1", ",", "n"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ";"}]}], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838202113291966*^9, 3.838202177869307*^9}, {
  3.838202212689951*^9, 3.838202222211845*^9}, {3.838202256794484*^9, 
  3.838202302223117*^9}, {3.8382030646149197`*^9, 3.838203078838071*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[2]:=",ExpressionUUID->"0d0a902b-1d74-4137-910a-ba56347077ae"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Initially, all agents are singles, therefore their initial utilities ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    SuperscriptBox["u", "k"], "t"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "987489bd-5be9-444a-9bbc-eac8a434ce91"],
 "will be given by the diagonal elements of the affinity matrix, i.e.,"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838202338374342*^9, 3.8382023598208447`*^9}, {
  3.838202974727859*^9, 3.838203001047039*^9}, {3.83820310905998*^9, 
  3.8382031164196653`*^9}, {3.8382100374435787`*^9, 
  3.838210046710848*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6dde6181-d879-4521-9029-\
5f21d71a726f"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Couples", "=", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"{", "}"}], "}"}]}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Utility", "=", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"A", "\[LeftDoubleBracket]", "k", "\[RightDoubleBracket]"}], 
       "\[LeftDoubleBracket]", "k", "\[RightDoubleBracket]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"k", ",", "1", ",", "n"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "}"}]}], 
  ";"}]}], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8382030027768106`*^9, 3.838203028073133*^9}, {
  3.838203156957013*^9, 3.838203158019259*^9}, {3.838203530907086*^9, 
  3.838203546548686*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[4]:=",ExpressionUUID->"6fa0bdba-e3c9-476d-b64d-03e6a878a3d6"],

Cell["\<\
The histogram with this values follows a Gaussian distribution and can be \
visualized easily using:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8382031222857533`*^9, 
  3.8382031427552156`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2b829d91-5296-4bed-8984-\
575cdaac7edb"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Histogram", "[", 
  RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"A", "\[LeftDoubleBracket]", "k", "\[RightDoubleBracket]"}], 
     "\[LeftDoubleBracket]", "k", "\[RightDoubleBracket]"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"k", ",", "1", ",", "n"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838202226858243*^9, 3.8382022629204063`*^9}, {
  3.838203145661756*^9, 3.838203161277339*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[6]:=",ExpressionUUID->"3f8320c3-ebeb-4d19-b16a-fda6823eb3c6"],

