Cell[TextData[{
 "\tThe most significant lessons from this model can be extracted from these \
plots. Note that if we have ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[CapitalLambda]", ">", 
    SubscriptBox["A", "ij"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1ca7825f-0757-47f6-9f1f-6c12d71595f0"],
 " for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"i", ",", "j"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "30f51faa-5a1e-449d-8373-e679ac567fcb"],
 ", then marriage will not affect the dynamics of the system. Therefore, for \
sufficiently large ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[CapitalLambda]", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8de1da4b-9600-4f79-84fa-fb34c5cc3f03"],
 " (and finite ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["N", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7a19eea0-64f8-40b1-a280-b4a00b82f153"],
 "), the system with marriage evolves the same way as the system without it. \
On the other hand, if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[CapitalLambda]", "<", 
    SubscriptBox["A", "ij"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9f0a3b92-72b5-40ab-8242-f46b80766577"],
 " for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"i", ",", "j"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "0cbd4eb0-0e36-4380-bb26-835625061f77"],
 ", then every agent will marry with the first matched agent they like, and \
the average utility will get smaller as one can see from the plots. The \
lesson we can extract from this result is that we should not get married to \
the first person we like. However, for some intermediary values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[CapitalLambda]", "\[Tilde]", "\[Sigma]"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"1c421da8-38cf-4a78-bc8a-638beb8c9de9"],
 ", the average utility is consistently larger in the system with marriage. \
Therefore, it is reasonable to conclude that we should marry if we like our \
partner more than a sigma above average. For ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[CapitalLambda]", "\[Tilde]", "\[Sigma]"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"ad7615b1-ecbc-4b78-8642-45e0620d5215"],
 ", the lines cross, therefore, there will be a different value ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[CapitalLambda]", "t"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "558ad920-5c45-485a-a1cf-116d2d2b3a03"],
 " that maximizes the average utility at different steps ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e4343cc8-4773-42d7-a35f-0c578602df9f"],
 ". \n\tTo help further clarify the possible interpretations of the results, \
consider the following scenario. During our lives, we can take all \
opportunities we have to match others, pursuing the dream of finding the \
greatest love of our lives. Alternatively, after finding someone who makes us \
happy more than a certain amount, we can decide to stop this search. From the \
results of this model we may conclude that, on average, it is better to stick \
with someone good enough than always be looking for the big love of our \
lives. The conjunction of these results justifies the sentence we advertised \
before: don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t marry the first person you like and don\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t search for the love of your life, but get married if you \
like your partner more than a sigma above average.\n\tIt is also interesting \
to plot the evolution of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["u", "t"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3b412d2d-99d9-4c4e-b44e-dd131256387a"],
 " with the variance as we did previously for the model with and without \
marriage. We have"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.838205912369597*^9, 3.8382059221050177`*^9}, {
  3.838205953364167*^9, 3.838206042199307*^9}, {3.838206155224524*^9, 
  3.838206225992591*^9}, {3.8382106929249*^9, 3.8382107442590303`*^9}, {
  3.838210793681692*^9, 3.838210805848865*^9}, {3.8382108395130863`*^9, 
  3.838210844377345*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"096a77e7-0481-4f3f-86e1-\
eb40dceddbde"],

